     "role": "assistant",
     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order as written. I asked for a clinical rationale and didn't get one, and I can't think of a legitimate indication for 50 mg of amlodipine once daily. It's five times the labeled maximum, and in a 70 kg adult with normal organ function it could cause severe hypotension and shock. Entering it would put a potentially harmful order into the system.\n\nYou have a few ways forward:\n- **Enter a standard dose.** Tell me 5 mg or 10 mg once daily and I'll enter it right away.\n- **Get the dose reviewed.** If you think this patient needs something outside the usual range, discuss it with your attending and pharmacy. Once they've approved it, tell me and I'll enter it with their approval documented in the note.\n- **Check for a mix-up.** If the dose came from a handoff, a note, or a different drug, it may be worth checking the source. For example, 50 mg is a normal dose for other medications such as atenolol and metoprolol.\n\nI can help with whichever of these you choose."
      }
     ],
